DESC = "Male voice in his 30s with an American accent, a warm natural documentary storyteller, conversational and sincere, clear mid pitch, medium pacing with natural pauses, engaged and curious, not acting."
PARAS = [
    # COLD OPEN
    ("When StubHub hid its fees until checkout, people spent about twenty one percent more. Same tickets. Same seats. The only thing that changed was when you saw the real price.", 0.4, 801),
    ("Economists ran that test on millions of real shoppers. And there's one industry that built its entire pricing around the same idea. Hotels.", 0.5, 802),
    # TRICK 1: THE HEADLINE PRICE
    ("Trick number one is the headline price. Here's how the StubHub test worked. Half of its US users saw the full price with fees from the start. The other half saw a lower price, and the fees only showed up at the very end.", 0.3, 803),
    ("The second group was fourteen percent more likely to buy, and they spent about twenty one percent more. Once you've picked your seats and you're at checkout, you don't want to start over. So you pay.", 0.3, 804),
    ("One of the economists behind it said, I can't think of a good reason to allow this practice in any country, as the harm to consumers is clear from our study.", 0.5, 805),
    # TRICK 2: THE RESORT FEE
    ("Trick number two is the resort fee. You find a room that looks cheap. Then, near the end, a daily fee shows up. Resort fee. Destination fee. Amenity fee.", 0.3, 806),
    ("On the Las Vegas Strip, the Review Journal found resort fees between about forty four and fifty seven dollars a night. A NerdWallet study of a hundred and sixty hotels found an average of about thirty three dollars a night, and about fifty dollars at Marriott hotels that charged one.", 0.3, 807),
    ("Do the math on a long weekend. Say the room shows up at ninety nine dollars a night. Add a fifty dollar resort fee, and three nights cost a hundred and fifty dollars more than the price that made you click. Before taxes.", 0.4, 821),
    ("Hotels say the fee covers things like Wi-Fi, the pool and the gym. Their industry group says these fees provide customers value by bundling unique property amenities. One traveler put it differently. An underhanded way to raise rates.", 0.5, 808),
    # TRICK 3: THE SORT ORDER
    ("Trick number three is about where you see the hotel in the first place. Before the new rules, when you sorted by price on a booking site, the hotel with the lower headline price showed up higher. So a hotel that moves more of its price into a fee looked cheaper than an honest hotel that didn't.", 0.3, 809),
    ("That's why this trick spread. If your competitor hides fifty dollars and you don't, you look fifty dollars more expensive. Even if you're actually cheaper.", 0.5, 810),
    # TRICK 4: THE MINIBAR
    ("Trick number four is waiting in the room. Many hotels now use minibars and snack trays with sensors. Pick something up, and it can land on your bill. In some, if you don't put it back within sixty seconds.", 0.3, 811),
    ("One reporter at a DoubleTree in Chicago found six minibar charges on his bill, between six and almost eight dollars each. His family hadn't eaten anything. One hotel manager estimated that as many as ninety percent of automatic minibar charges are mistakes.", 0.5, 812),
    # TRICK 5: THE LAW CATCHES UP
    ("And then, the rules started to change. Pennsylvania sued Marriott over resort fees, and in the settlement, Marriott agreed to include them in the price it shows you.", 0.3, 813),
    ("In May twenty twenty five, a Federal Trade Commission rule took effect. Hotels, vacation rentals and ticket sellers now have to show the total price up front, with mandatory fees included, and more prominently than any other price.", 0.3, 814),
    ("But read that carefully. The rule doesn't ban the fees. It just makes them show up earlier. The fifty dollar resort fee is still there. It just can't hide anymore.", 0.5, 815),
    # WHAT TO DO
    ("So here's how you beat it.", 0.3, 816),
    ("Compare the total price for your whole stay, never the nightly rate. If you have hotel points, Hilton and Hyatt waive resort fees on stays booked with points. If a fee shows up that you weren't told about, complain at the front desk early, not at checkout. And if you were quoted a lower rate in writing, a travel consumer advocate says you can dispute the charge with your credit card company.", 0.3, 817),
    ("And the minibar? Don't touch it. Not even to look at the price.", 0.5, 818),
    ("The cheapest looking price is a trick. The total is the price.", 0.5, 819),
    ("We break down one business trick every day. Subscribe, and tell us the worst hidden fee you've ever paid.", 0.0, 820),
]
